# Negative-control mutant — the grouped-count read's fixed ordering (AC-19, R36)
#
# Scenario it must flip: contract-product.feature >
#   "See my products grouped by category, with a count for each"
#   (the dashboard grouped-count read carries the `service_identifier`
#   ordering the design fixes for it)
# Test that goes red: contract-product.reads.int.test.ts >
#   the grouped-counts wire assertion — the observed
#   `GET clients/{id}/contracts/products` request carries `order=service_identifier`.
#   The load-bearing assertion: that `order` param is present and equal to
#   `service_identifier`.
# Mutation: drop the `sort` DEFAULT from `useGroupedCountsQuerySchema()`. The
#   read parses an EMPTY model against the schema, so the default IS the sort:
#   with it gone the parsed model carries no `sort`, `translateQuery` emits no
#   tuple, and `useQuery`'s request builder DELETES the `order` key. The
#   grouped read then reaches the wire unordered and the scenario goes red.
#
# Why the DEFAULT, not the field enum: the field enum
#   (`enum: ["service_identifier"]`) is what DECLARES the column orderable, so
#   `declaredSortFields` keeps it through `translateQuery`; the `default` is
#   what SUPPLIES the one entry against an empty model. Removing the enum would
#   instead reject the value at parse time — a different failure mode. This
#   mutant targets the seam R36 names: the schema's own sort default.
#
# KNOWN GAP: as of this pass no wire assertion drives `loadGroupedCounts` in
#   `contract-product.reads.int.test.ts` (AC-19 is a registered @gap —
#   `contract-product.feature:305/306`, no capture on disk). A stub handler on
#   `clients/{id}/contracts/products` returning `[]` and reading back
#   `order=service_identifier` needs no recorded capture and is the prover-seat
#   assertion this control is authored to flip. Until it lands this mutant is
#   inert, by test coverage — not by construction.
#
# Apply with:  git apply <this file>   (run from repo root)
# Revert with: git apply -R <this file>
